    id: "4ba6b51e197120532e00",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Brazil's Lula bans online gambling ahead of presidential election",
    summary: "Lula, who is currently in a tight presidential race against Flávio Bolsonaro, said the industry was a \"cancer\" that he must \"remove\".",
    preview: "Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva has announced a ban on online gambling, just days before an election in which he is seeking a fourth term as the country's leader. Lula, who is running against the son of the incarcerated former Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro, signed a provisional order on Friday that \"prohibits all sports betting and online casinos\". The online gambling industry in Brazil was legalised in 2018 and has since been blamed for widespread household income losses due to addiction.",
    body: "Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva has announced a ban on online gambling, just days before an election in which he is seeking a fourth term as the country's leader.\n\nLula, who is running against the son of the incarcerated former Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro, signed a provisional order on Friday that \"prohibits all sports betting and online casinos\".\n\nThe online gambling industry in Brazil was legalised in 2018 and has since been blamed for widespread household income losses due to addiction.\n\nLula described the industry as a \"cancer\" in his announcement, adding \"either we remove the tumour, or it kills us\".\n\nThe move comes just over a week before the first round of voting in Brazil's presidential election. Recent polls have the 80-year-old leader almost neck-and-neck with Flávio Bolsonaro.\n\nThe new measures must receive congressional approval within 120 days to remain in force.\n\nLula acknowledged online betting was created under the former President Michel Temer's administration but that it \"grew without regulation under Bolsonaro's management\", in a statement on X .\n\nHe said that there were \"no restrictions\" particularly for preventing children and adolescents from accessing online betting.\n\n\"We won't allow betting companies to profit at the expense of Brazilian families' income and lives,\" the president added.\n\nThe president described how he had heard from families who had sold everything in their houses because of their gambling addictions.\n\nSeparate to the online betting order, Lula also signed a bill to penalise criminal offences related to fixed-odds betting.\n\nEarlier this week, at the United Nations General Assembly, Lula accused the industry of \"transforming addiction into profit\".\n\nBut Flávio Bolsonaro called the gambling crackdown \"populist, hypocritical and politically motivated\" during a campaign rally in Rio de Janeiro on Friday, according to Reuters.\n\nMost types of gambling, such as casinos and slot machines, are illegal in Brazil, but ever since online betting was legalised in 2018, it has exploded in popularity.\n\nAbout 59.9% of Brazilian adults support banning internet betting, a survey by Atlas and Bloomberg published this week has found.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published18 August\n\n- Published13 August",
